Certainly! Dynamic Programming (DP) can significantly improve the efficiency of solving problems with overlapping subproblems or optimal substructure properties. Naive recursive approaches often lead to redundant computations, which can make the solution inefficient.

For instance, consider the Fibonacci sequence where each number is the sum of the two preceding ones. A naive recursive approach would recompute the same values multiple times, leading to exponential time complexity.

Dynamic Programming helps by storing intermediate results to avoid recomputation.

### Example: Calculating Fibonacci Numbers

Here’s how you might implement a naive and DP approach:

#### Naive Recursive Approach:
```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

long long fib(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return n;
    return fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
}

int main() {
    int n = 40;
    cout << "Fibonacci(" << n << ") = " << fib(n) << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
This approach has a time complexity of \(O(2^n)\).

